# 🚀 Deployment

This project ships as a Docker image via `docker-compose.prod.yml`. The deploy pipeline only updates the `DOCKER_IMAGE` line in `.env` — it never creates `.env`. So before your **first** deploy, create `.env` on the server by hand, with the keys below.

{{#if (eq production.mode "proxied")}}
## 🤖 Automated setup (beta)

> **⚠️ Beta** — `next-suite provision` is new and experimental: it changes a real server (creates a user, writes nginx config, obtains a certificate, sets the GitHub secrets below). Run `next-suite provision --dry-run` first to see the exact commands.

`next-suite provision` comes from installing the CLI globally: `npm i -g create-next-suite@latest`. Run `next-suite provision` from this project directory to perform the server setup below automatically — server user, `.env`, nginx + TLS, and the GitHub secrets/variables. `next-suite deprovision` rolls it back. The manual steps below remain the fallback/reference.

{{/if}}
## 📋 Required `.env` keys

```dotenv
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME={{projectName}}
{{#if (eq production.mode "proxied")}}
APP_PORT=8100
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq database.engine "postgres")}}
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_HOST=postgres
POSTGRES_USER=next
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-before-production
POSTGRES_DATABASE={{projectName}}
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq database.engine "mysql")}}
MYSQL_PORT=3306
MYSQL_HOST=mysql
MYSQL_USER=root
MYSQL_PASSWORD=change-me-before-production
MYSQL_DATABASE={{projectName}}
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq auth "better-auth")}}
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=generate-with-openssl-rand-base64-32
{{/if}}
{{#if (or api (eq auth "better-auth"))}}
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://your-domain.example
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq email "resend")}}
RESEND_API_KEY=from-https-resend-com
EMAIL_FROM=App <onboarding@resend.dev>
{{/if}}
```

Generate a secret with `openssl rand -base64 32`.

{{#if (eq production.mode "proxied")}}
`APP_PORT` is the loopback port the nginx sidecar publishes; it must match the `proxy_pass` port in the host's `/etc/nginx/conf.d/<name>.conf`. `next-suite provision` assigns and sets it for you. Compose refuses to start without it rather than falling back to a port another service may already own.

### 🤝 What the host proxy must send

The sidecar binds `127.0.0.1` only, so **the host proxy is the trust boundary** — the sidecar forwards these headers rather than setting them, and the app trusts what arrives. `next-suite provision` writes a host block that gets this right; a hand-written one must too:

```nginx
proxy_set_header Host              $host;
proxy_set_header X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For   $remote_addr;   # NOT $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for —
                                                   # that appends the client's own value
proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host  $host;          # NOT $http_x_forwarded_host: Next.js
                                                   # compares it against Origin for
                                                   # Server Action CSRF
client_max_body_size 25m;                          # host default is 1m and would 413 first
```

Get it wrong and the failures are quiet rather than loud: with no `X-Forwarded-For` the app logs the proxy's IP for every request and any IP-based rate limiting collapses to a single bucket; with `Host` left at nginx's `$proxy_host` default every absolute URL the app derives points at `127.0.0.1`.

A missing `X-Forwarded-Proto` is the one case the sidecar covers on its own — a `map` defaults it to `https`, so a forgetful proxy cannot make the app think it is on plain HTTP and drop the `Secure` flag from auth cookies.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq production.mode "standalone")}}
> **Certificate renewal.** This container terminates TLS itself, so a host-level `systemctl reload nginx` does not reach it — nginx keeps serving the certificate it read at startup. Have certbot's deploy hook signal the container as well:
>
> ```sh
> docker ps -q --filter label=com.docker.compose.service=nginx | xargs -r docker kill -s HUP
> ```
>
> Verify with `certbot renew --dry-run --run-deploy-hooks` (plain `--dry-run` skips deploy hooks).
{{/if}}

{{#if (includes githubActions "deploy")}}
## 🔐 GitHub Actions secrets & variables

The `CD` workflow deploys over SSH — set these in the repo (**Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions**):

**Secrets**

- `DEPLOY_SSH_KEY` — private SSH key authorized on the server
- `DEPLOY_SSH_HOST` — server hostname or IP
- `DEPLOY_SSH_USER` — SSH user

> **⚠️ `DEPLOY_SSH_KEY` is a root-equivalent credential.** The deploy user is in
> the `docker` group so it can run `docker compose` without `sudo` — and docker
> group access is equivalent to root on the host (`docker run -v /:/host …`).
> Anyone with write access to this repository, any compromised third-party
> action, and any workflow that runs on untrusted input can therefore reach
> root on the server, including every other project on it.
>
> Keep the key out of `pull_request` workflows from forks, and prefer an
> environment with required reviewers for the deploy job.
>
> To rotate it: delete `~/.config/next-suite/keys/<project>` (and `.pub`), re-run
> `next-suite provision` to mint and upload a new one — then remove the **old**
> public key from `/srv/www/<project>/.ssh/authorized_keys` by hand. provision
> only appends, so without that last step the retired key keeps working.

**Variables**

- `DEPLOY_PATH` — absolute path to the deploy directory
{{#if (or api (eq auth "better-auth"))}}
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` — the app's public URL, **baked into the image at build time**

> Set this **before the first push**, or the image is built with it unset and the
> app fails env validation on every request — while the container still reports
> healthy. Setting it in the server `.env` afterwards does not help: Next inlines
> `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` at build time, it is not read at runtime. `next-suite provision`
> sets it; if you pushed first, rebuild the image afterwards.
{{/if}}

{{/if}}
## 🚦 First deploy

{{#if (eq production.mode "standalone")}}
This stack terminates TLS itself, so two things must exist **before** the first
deploy — nginx refuses to start without them and the container crash-loops.

1. **Put your domain into `nginx/nginx.conf`.** Four places are marked
   `# TODO`: the `server_name` in the `:80` and the `:443` block, and the two
   `ssl_certificate*` paths. The shipped value is `example.com`.

   While you are there: the `Strict-Transport-Security` line ships **without**
   `includeSubDomains`, deliberately. Add it only once you know this domain is a
   subdomain whose siblings all speak HTTPS — on an apex it commits **every**
   subdomain of the zone for two years, with no server-side way back.

2. **Obtain the certificate.** The ACME challenge is served by this same nginx,
   which cannot start yet, so the first certificate has to come from outside the
   stack. With ports 80/443 still free:

   ```bash
   certbot certonly --standalone -d <your-domain>
   ```

   Renewals are different — by then nginx is running and serves the webroot, so
   the deploy hook above is what keeps them working.

3. On the server, create `<deploy-path>/.env` with real values for the keys
   above — or let the shipped script do it:
   `bash scripts/prod.sh bootstrap`.
4. Trigger the deploy (push to `main`{{#if (includes githubActions "deploy")}}, or run the `CD` workflow manually{{/if}}) — it ships `docker-compose.prod.yml`, `nginx`, and `scripts`, then pulls the built image.
{{else}}
1. On the server, create `<deploy-path>/.env` with real values for the keys above — or let the shipped script do it: `bash scripts/prod.sh bootstrap`.
2. Trigger the deploy (push to `main`{{#if (includes githubActions "deploy")}}, or run the `CD` workflow manually{{/if}}) — it ships `docker-compose.prod.yml`, `nginx`, and `scripts`, then pulls the built image.
{{/if}}

## 📡 Streaming responses

nginx cuts a proxied connection after 120s without data (`proxy_read_timeout`). Streaming SSR is unaffected — it keeps flushing — but an idle Server-Sent-Events or WebSocket connection is not. Keep them alive from the app rather than raising the timeout: send an SSE comment (`: ping\n\n`) or a WebSocket ping every 25–30s.

`X-Accel-Buffering: no` — the usual advice — does **not** help here: nginx consumes `X-Accel-*` rather than forwarding it, so it never reaches the hop that would act on it. It is also unnecessary, because the location serving the app runs with `proxy_buffering off` already; only the static-asset locations buffer, and those serve finite files.

## 🛠️ Operating the app

The deploy also ships `scripts/prod.sh`:

```bash
bash scripts/prod.sh bootstrap  # create .env from .env.example + generate the auth secret
bash scripts/prod.sh up       # migrate, then start (builds the image locally if none is set)
bash scripts/prod.sh logs     # follow logs
bash scripts/prod.sh down     # stop
bash scripts/prod.sh restart  # restart
bash scripts/prod.sh ps       # status
```
{{#if database}}

`up` applies pending migrations **before** it touches the stack, in a
short-lived `migrate` container. That ordering is deliberate: `docker compose
up` stops the running containers to recreate them, so a migration that fails
during `up` leaves the site down — run on its own it fails while the current
release is still serving, and nothing has been changed.
{{/if}}
